Best practice is to keep it. Nearly every Python style guide recommends it for any file that both defines functions and runs something.

What it means

Python sets a built-in variable called __name__ in every file it loads:

If you run the file directly (python intake_safe.py), __name__ is "__main__".
If another file imports it (from intake_safe import parse_row), __name__ is "intake_safe".
So if __name__ == "__main__": reads as "only do this part when I was run directly, not when someone borrowed my functions."

Why it's the right way

Your file has two jobs: it defines tools (parse_row, load_cases, report), and it runs them on the CSV. The guard separates the two. Without it:

Importing the file for its functions also loads the CSV and prints the report.
Anything that imports it depends on the working directory, which is how a test run from the repo root would crash.
You can't reuse the functions on a different file without side effects.
For this assignment

The grader only runs the script and pytest from week06, and both work either way. So removing it won't cost you points here. It is a habit worth building, though, since later weeks will import your earlier code.

