I'll use four real rows from your file: lines 2, 3, 17 and 26. The full file has 100 rows, but these four show every path.

Phase 1: the caller starts things off

good, quarantine = load_cases("wk06_data_raw_cases_100.csv")
Execution jumps into load_cases. The caller waits.

Phase 2: inside load_cases
It starts with good = [] and quarantine = []. Then next(reader) consumes the header line, so the loop sees only data rows.

Line 2: CC-20000,"DELGADO, PATRICE",M,60,,6115 scyene road,326,CLOSED

csv.reader splits it into 8 fields. The quoted comma stays inside the name.
parse_row passes the length check, and int("60") works.
row[4] is "", so it raises ValueError("missing date").
The except catches it and runs quarantine.append((2, 'missing date', 'CC-20000,DELGADO, PATRICE,M,60,,...')).
State: good = [], quarantine = [1 item]

Line 3: CC-20001,"OKAFOR, TASHA",M,61,2011-10-24,...

Every check passes, so parse_row returns a dict with the name cleaned to Okafor, Tasha.
good.append(...) adds it.
State: good = [1 item], quarantine = [1 item]

Line 17: CC-20015,"REYES, ALICIA",M,unknown,2009-06-08,...

The length check passes, then int("unknown") fails on its own with a ValueError.
It's caught and quarantined with the reason invalid literal for int()....
State: good = [1], quarantine = [2]

Line 26: CC-20024,"PALACIOS, FELIX",M,38,2002-10-02,5928 peavy road

It has only 6 fields, so len(row) != 8 raises "expected 8 fields, got 6".
It's quarantined.
State: good = [1], quarantine = [3]

The loop keeps going through the other 96 rows. At the end, the real totals are good with 89 dicts and quarantine with 11 tuples.

Phase 3: the return
return good, quarantine sends both lists back. load_cases ends and its local names vanish. The caller stores the lists in its own good and quarantine.

Phase 4: the caller passes them to report

report(good, quarantine)
report's parameters now point at those same two lists. Inside report:


Rows loaded cleanly:   89
Rows quarantined:      11

QUARANTINE LOG - every rejected row, with the reason:
  line 2: missing date | CC-20000,DELGADO, PATRICE...
  line 17: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'unknown' | CC-20015,REYES, ALICIA,M,...
  line 26: expected 8 fields, got 6 | CC-20024,PALACIOS, FELIX,M...
  ...
Each quarantine entry is (line_num, reason, raw), which is why the for line_num, reason, raw in quarantine: loop can unpack it into three variables. The raw[:25] slice cuts each row off after 25 characters.

Notice that the line-2 tuple was created in Phase 2 and is printed in Phase 4 by a different function. That works because the data travelled through the return and then through the caller's argument.



