I think the confusion is that the file's order doesn't match the order things run. If that's not what you meant, tell me which part and I'll redo it.

The order the file is written in, top to bottom:

parse_row (line 13)
load_cases (line 39)
report (line 70)
The if __name__ block (line 95)
The order Python runs it:

Python reads top to bottom, but the three def lines only store the functions. Nothing inside them runs yet.
It reaches the if __name__ block at the bottom. This is where the program actually starts.
Line 96 calls load_cases, so execution jumps up to line 39.
Inside load_cases, line 53 calls parse_row, so execution jumps up to line 13. It does this once for each row, 100 times.
parse_row finishes and execution returns to line 53.
load_cases finishes at line 58 and execution returns to line 96.
Line 97 calls report, so execution jumps up to line 70.
report finishes and the program ends.
As a trace of line numbers:


95 → 96 → 39 → (48 → 53 → 13 → ... back to 53 → ...) ×100 → 58 → 96 → 97 → 70 → ... → end
The file reads like a story with parse_row first, but the execution starts at the bottom and jumps up into the functions as they're called. That's why my walkthrough seemed to jump around: it followed the execution order, not the order of the file.

Is that the mismatch you meant, or did you mean something else, like the comments in the file?